  <!-- The IPA members below are the strict phonetic syllabary used by the
       Mandarin worked syllabifier. Tone is absent because it rides the
       syllable and does not change membership. Additions belong here, at the
       Pinyin declaration boundary, rather than in Python.

       This list is a declared demonstrator, not a partial copy of the real
       syllabary. Hanyu Pinyin has on the order of four hundred syllables and
       a dozen are declared here, which is a stance rather than neglect:
       syllabification is optional in this library, only the operations that
       need it depend on it, and most lexicons supply none at all. A syllable
       the list does not hold is REPORTED rather than guessed, so
       `syllabify("pʰiŋ", "mandarin")` answers with no syllables and
       `unsyllabified=((0, 3),)`, which is a legible refusal and not a silent
       wrong answer. The sibling kana declaration takes the same
       attested only position.

       It grows when a consumer needs it, and growing it is curation rather
       than pasting a table. The house pattern is the one
       `scripts/syllable_curation.py` used for the English onsets, where each
       entry carries a harvested count, an exemplar and a decision. Anything
       added here has to face where a naive table goes wrong: the syllabic
       fricatives of zhi, chi, shi, ri, zi, ci and si; the y and w spelling
       rules behind yi, wu, yu, ye and yuan; `er`; and tone riding the
       syllable without changing membership. -->
